window.MR.alaska={"_meta":{"captured":"2026-09-29","what_this_is":"A one-page Alaska dossier: every named opportunity, precedent contract, and rule this site has documented for Alaska, in one place. It draws its opportunity and precedent rows from opportunities.json, strategy.json, deployment_sites.json, regions.json, benchmarks.json and costs.json (already cited there) and adds three things not tracked elsewhere: Alaska's statewide generation mix, which kind of buyer a reactor would actually be signing with, and the state law that governs behind-the-meter and cooperative generation.","method":"Opportunity and precedent rows are pulled programmatically at build time from the site's existing Alaska-tagged records (tools/build_data.py:build_alaska_page()), not retyped here. This file holds only new synthesis: the buyer-model taxonomy, the grid-isolation note on each opportunity, and the power-mix and regulatory findings, each with its own primary source.","opportunity_rows":[{"id":"cvea-valdez","site_id":"cvea-valdez","prospect_id":"cvea","buyer_model":"cooperative","grid_note":"Islanded cooperative grid, now building a Railbelt intertie via the Alyeska Valdez Marine Terminal"},{"id":"eielson-daf","site_id":"eielson-pilot","prospect_id":"eielson-daf","buyer_model":"federal-installation","grid_note":"On the Railbelt; the base buys its electricity from Golden Valley Electric Association today"},{"id":"fort-wainwright-doyon","site_id":"fort-wainwright-doyon","prospect_id":"doyon-utilities","buyer_model":"federal-installation","grid_note":"Near the Railbelt but self-contained: Doyon operates the installation's own coal-fired combined heat and power plant, not a GVEA connection"},{"id":"red-dog","site_id":"red-dog-mine","prospect_id":"red-dog","buyer_model":"behind-the-fence-industrial","grid_note":"Off-grid; the mine runs its own diesel power station with no utility of record"},{"id":"graphite-creek","site_id":"graphite-creek","prospect_id":null,"buyer_model":"behind-the-fence-industrial","grid_note":"Off-grid; the feasibility study's base case is the mine's own diesel generators, not a utility connection"},{"id":"tanana-chiefs","site_id":null,"prospect_id":"tanana-chiefs","buyer_model":"cooperative","grid_note":"Eight isolated village microgrids with no interconnection between them; no single village comes close to a microreactor's minimum output on its own"}]},"buyer_models":[{"id":"cooperative","label":"Member-owned cooperative","examples":["cvea","tanana-chiefs"],"what_it_means":"The utility is owned by the people it serves and run by an elected board. It is regulated by the RCA as a public utility like any other, but a cooperative gets one thing an investor-owned utility does not: the right to raise base rates up to 8% a year (20% over 3 years) through a Simplified Rate Filing instead of a full rate case. No Alaska statute or RCA rule found in this research requires a membership vote before a co-op adds a generation asset — that decision sits with the elected board.","sale_shape":"Cooperative ownership of the reactor, in the rate base, beat vendor ownership on member economics in CVEA's own pre-feasibility study — the study's stated reason for recommending member ownership over a vendor-owned unit.","watch_for":"A tabled cooperative project (CVEA, 2023) does not mean the law or the site died — it means the board judged the specific unit uneconomic at the time. The siting law and the site itself are unchanged.","sources":[{"label":"CVEA / Ruralite (September 2023) — Micro-Modular Nuclear Reactor Project Tabled","url":"https://www.cvea.org/assets/documents/pdfs/ruralite-articles/2023/september.pdf","quote":"Those included Westinghouse, OKLO, NuScale, & Radiant Technologies","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Alaska Energy Transparency Project — Rate Case explainer","url":"https://www.akenergytransparency.org/rate-case","quote":"Cooperatives can increase base rates by up to 8% a year or 20% every 3 years through the Simplified Rate Filing (SRF) process","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"municipal-utility","label":"Municipal or borough utility","examples":[],"what_it_means":"A town-owned utility answers to its city council or a utility board rather than a membership vote — and by default it sets its own rates outside RCA review. AS 42.05.711(b) exempts a utility owned and operated by a political subdivision from Chapter 42.05 except for the certification provisions (AS 42.05.221–42.05.281) and AS 42.05.385; a town keeps that exemption unless its own governing body elects to be regulated. This site's Regions tab documents Nome, Kotzebue, Bethel and Utqiagvik each running their own municipal or cooperative diesel utility outside the Alaska Village Electric Cooperative system.","sale_shape":"A single-town buyer is small, so a reactor sized for one town has to clear that town's own diesel cost, not a statewide average — and PCE-community rates documented in this site's Regions tab range 20x community to community. Whether a reactor deal needs a CPCN still applies (certification is not exempted), but the ongoing rate itself may be set locally rather than by RCA tariff order, unless the town opted in.","watch_for":"No named microreactor prospect at a municipal utility has been documented yet; this row states the model, not a live deal. Confirm whether the specific town has elected RCA regulation before assuming either rate path.","sources":[{"label":"FindLaw — Alaska Statutes Title 42 Sec. 42.05.711 (Exemptions)","url":"https://codes.findlaw.com/ak/title-42-public-utilities-and-carriers-and-energy-programs/ak-st-sect-42-05-711/","quote":"public utilities owned and operated by a political subdivision of the state","status":"fetched"},{"label":"This site — Applications · Regions · Rural Alaska (Power Cost Equalization communities)","url":"https://pranava0x0.github.io/microreactors/#demand/regions","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"railbelt-utility","label":"Larger interconnected utility (the Railbelt)","examples":["eielson-daf"],"what_it_means":"Six interconnected utilities from Fairbanks to the Kenai Peninsula (GVEA, Chugach, Matanuska, Homer Electric, Anchorage's municipal successor, and Seward) share one grid and buy wholesale power under filed PPAs, the same instrument a reactor developer would use.","sale_shape":"GVEA's own filed PPA amendment with Aurora Energy is the price a reactor has to beat on the Railbelt: $115.23/MWh in 2026, up from $94.23/MWh, for firm dispatchable power — not a diesel number, a real wholesale-power filing.","watch_for":"No Railbelt utility has issued an RFI or committed capital to a reactor study; the Alaska Center for Energy and Power's May 2026 tour of Idaho National Laboratory with GVEA staff is interest, not a procurement.","sources":[{"label":"State of Alaska / RCA - TA401-13 GVEA-Aurora tariff filing notice (2026)","url":"https://aws.state.ak.us/OnlinePublicNotices/Notices/View.aspx?id=222446","quote":"1 sets a new base rate for 2026 of $115.23/MWh, an increase from the current 2026 rate of $94.23/MWh.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"federal-installation","label":"Federal installation (direct PPA or a privatized-utility contract)","examples":["eielson-daf","fort-wainwright-doyon"],"what_it_means":"Two different federal buying models exist side by side in interior Alaska. Eielson AFB buys under a direct 30-year firm-fixed-price PPA to a central buying agency (DLA Energy), with the vendor owning and operating the unit. Fort Wainwright's utilities are run under a long-term utility-privatization contract by Doyon, an Alaska Native corporation joint venture, with rates set by the RCA — a reactor there would enter as a rate-based plant addition, not a PPA.","sale_shape":"Eielson: owner-operator PPA to DLA Energy, NRC licensing first. Fort Wainwright: a plant addition inside an existing privatized-utility rate base, requiring DLA, Doyon and RCA sign-off, not a new contract vehicle.","watch_for":"Neither is a signed reactor deal yet. Eielson has a Notice of Intent to Award (NOITA) to Oklo, pending NRC licensing. Fort Wainwright is an unpaired Janus candidate with no vendor match as of the 2026-08-26 Army announcement.","sources":[{"label":"DAF — Eielson microreactor project page and 2026 updates","url":"https://www.eielson.af.mil/microreactor/","quote":"If ultimately awarded the contract","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"behind-the-fence-industrial","label":"Private industrial host (behind the fence)","examples":["red-dog","graphite-creek"],"what_it_means":"A mine or industrial site that already generates its own power on its own land can buy a reactor the way it buys a diesel plant: a term contract for power delivered behind the meter. This site's new legal research on AS 42.05 found no named “behind-the-meter” exemption in Alaska law — a site like this most plausibly stays outside RCA jurisdiction because it was never inside a certificated utility's territory and never sold power “to the public,” not because of a specific carve-out.","sale_shape":"Third-party-owned generation behind the fence under a term contract; at Red Dog specifically the unit would need to be relocatable, since the mine's closure is planned around 2032 and only a movable unit outlives the site.","watch_for":"This is the model with the fewest regulatory steps on paper, but the legal basis for staying outside RCA jurisdiction is an inference, not a codified rule — see Rules below — and it is also the smallest buyer: a single mine's remaining life, not a utility's multi-decade rate base.","sources":[{"label":"ArcticToday - One of Alaska's flagship mines soon could draw energy from the sun","url":"https://www.arctictoday.com/one-of-alaskas-flagship-mines-soon-could-draw-energy-from-the-sun/","quote":"8.8-megawatt solar farm","status":"fetched"}]}],"power_mix":{"statewide":{"id":"power-mix-statewide","label":"Statewide, 2024","total_generation_mwh":6690506,"primary_source":"Natural gas","note":"One statewide number hides three very different grids: see the regional split below. EIA does not publish a single fetchable statewide fuel-percentage table for 2024, so the regional breakdown uses the 2021 EIA/FERC-Form-1 data compiled by UAF's Alaska Center for Energy and Power, the most recent year with a full three-region split.","sources":[{"label":"EIA — Alaska Electricity Profile 2024","url":"https://www.eia.gov/electricity/state/alaska/","quote":"Net generation (megawatthours) 6,690,506","status":"fetched"}]},"regions":[{"id":"power-mix-railbelt","label":"Railbelt (Fairbanks to the Kenai Peninsula)","avg_annual_gwh_2011_2021":5109,"mix_2021":{"gas":61,"coal":15,"hydro":12,"oil":10,"wind":2},"note":"The interconnected grid GVEA, Chugach, Matanuska, Homer Electric, Anchorage's municipal successor and Seward share; see Regions → Alaska Railbelt for the utility-level detail.","sources":[{"label":"ACEP/UAF — 2024 Alaska Electricity Trends Report, Ch. 4 Net/Gross Generation","url":"https://acep-uaf.github.io/aetr-web-book-2024/generation.html","quote":"In 2021, the region generated 61% of electricity from gas, 15% from coal, 12% from hydro, 10% from oil, and 2% from wind.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"power-mix-coastal","label":"Coastal (Southeast and South-central hydro systems)","avg_annual_gwh_2011_2021":1131,"mix_2021":{"hydro":92,"oil":6,"wind":2},"note":"Already the state's cleanest region by far; not where a 24/7 diesel-replacement case is strongest.","sources":[{"label":"ACEP/UAF — 2024 Alaska Electricity Trends Report, Ch. 4 Net/Gross Generation","url":"https://acep-uaf.github.io/aetr-web-book-2024/generation.html","quote":"In 2021, the region generated 92% of electricity from hydro, 6% from oil, and 2% from wind.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"power-mix-rural-remote","label":"Rural Remote (off-road-system microgrids)","avg_annual_gwh_2011_2021":529,"mix_2021":{"oil":77,"gas":19,"hydro":1,"wind":3},"mix_2011":{"oil":74,"gas":24,"hydro":1,"wind":2},"note":"The smallest region by total generation and the most diesel-dependent — and it got more diesel-dependent, not less, over the decade EIA has comparable data for. This is the 24/7-diesel band the rest of this page's rural rows (AVEC, Tanana Chiefs) sit inside.","sources":[{"label":"ACEP/UAF — 2024 Alaska Electricity Trends Report, Ch. 4 Net/Gross Generation","url":"https://acep-uaf.github.io/aetr-web-book-2024/generation.html","quote":"In 2021, the region generated 77% of electricity from oil (mostly diesel), 19% from gas, 1% from hydro, and 3% from wind.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"ACEP/UAF — 2024 Alaska Electricity Trends Report, Ch. 4 Net/Gross Generation (2011 comparison)","url":"https://acep-uaf.github.io/aetr-web-book-2024/generation.html","quote":"In 2011, the region generated 74% of electricity from oil, 24% from gas, 1% from hydro, and 2% from wind.","status":"fetched"}]}]},"regulatory_notes":[{"id":"public-utility-threshold","topic":"Does a reactor need a state utility certificate?","finding":"Alaska's Regulatory Commission of Alaska (RCA) only has jurisdiction over a “public utility” — defined in AS 42.05.990(6) as an entity furnishing electrical service “to the public for compensation.” A public utility may not operate or charge for service without first getting a Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity (CPCN) from the RCA under AS 42.05.221. Alaska's statute does not name a bright-line “behind-the-meter” or “single customer” exemption; the closest carve-outs are AS 42.05.711(j), which exempts sales of waste heat or by-product industrial energy to an already-certificated utility, and AS 42.05.711(r), a capped exemption for plants generating entirely from renewable resources (up to 65 MW, in service 1990–2028, selling only to a regulated utility) that a reactor could not use because nuclear is not a listed renewable resource. The statute's own definition of “public” also reaches further than one customer: AS 42.05.990(5)(B) counts even a single customer as “the public” if it buys power for use inside an area a certificated electric utility presently or formerly served and pays that utility over $50,000 a year — so a reactor supplying one customer inside an existing utility's territory does not obviously escape certification the way a true greenfield, never-certificated site like Red Dog's own mine-site plant appears to.","figures":{"cpcn_statute":"AS 42.05.221","public_utility_definition":"AS 42.05.990(6)","public_definition_single_customer_test":"AS 42.05.990(5)(B): one customer counts as “the public” if inside a utility's presently-or-formerly-certificated area and paying over $50,000/year","renewable_exemption_cap_mw":65,"renewable_exemption_window":"1990-08-31 to 2028-07-01, sale only to a regulated utility — does not cover nuclear","de_minimis_exemption":"AS 42.05.711(e): any electric utility grossing under $50,000/year is exempt from the whole chapter"},"read":"The safest reading is model-specific, not statewide: a reactor built on a site that was never inside any certificated utility's territory (Red Dog's own mine-site plant, or a genuinely new industrial site) has the strongest case for staying outside RCA jurisdiction entirely, because it never meets the “to the public” threshold in the first place. A reactor selling behind the meter to one customer inside an existing utility's service area is on much less certain ground and should assume it needs a CPCN unless the RCA says otherwise.","sources":[{"label":"FindLaw — Alaska Statutes Title 42 Sec. 42.05.990 (public utility definition)","url":"https://codes.findlaw.com/ak/title-42-public-utilities-and-carriers-and-energy-programs/ak-st-sect-42-05-990.html","quote":"that owns, operates, manages, or controls any plant, pipeline, or system for","status":"fetched"},{"label":"law.onecle.com — Alaska Statutes Title 42.05 Art. 2, Sec. 42.05.221 (Certificates required)","url":"https://law.onecle.com/alaska/title-42/chapter-42.05/article-02/index.html","quote":"A public utility may not operate and receive compensation for providing a commodity or service without first having obtained from the commission under","status":"fetched"},{"label":"FindLaw — Alaska Statutes Title 42 Sec. 42.05.711 (Exemptions)","url":"https://codes.findlaw.com/ak/title-42-public-utilities-and-carriers-and-energy-programs/ak-st-sect-42-05-711/","quote":"does not generate more than 65 megawatts of electricity","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"cooperative-rate-flexibility","topic":"Is a cooperative buyer different from an investor-owned utility?","finding":"The clearest documented difference is rate flexibility, not the certification process for new generation. Under 3 AAC 48.500–560, a cooperative like AVEC or CVEA can raise its base rates up to 8% in any 12-month period, or 20% over 3 years, through the RCA's Simplified Rate Filing (SRF) process without a full rate case; an investor-owned utility has no equivalent shortcut and must file a full rate case for any base-rate change. A cooperative loses SRF access and is frozen at its interim rate level once it enters a full rate case. No statute or RCA guidance was found stating that a cooperative follows a lighter CPCN process than an IOU when adding new generation specifically, and no member-vote requirement for adding a generation asset to the rate base was found in a fetchable primary source — the governance lever that is documented is the elected member board plus SRF, not a formal generation-specific vote.","figures":{"srf_annual_cap_pct":8,"srf_three_year_cap_pct":20,"srf_regulation":"3 AAC 48.500-560"},"read":"Treat “cooperative members get a vote on the reactor” as CVEA's own board practice, not a codified state requirement — the one hard rule this research found is that co-ops raise rates faster than IOUs can, not that they decide generation differently.","sources":[{"label":"Alaska Energy Transparency Project — Rate Case explainer","url":"https://www.akenergytransparency.org/rate-case","quote":"Cooperatives can increase base rates by up to 8% a year or 20% every 3 years through the Simplified Rate Filing (SRF) process","status":"fetched"}]}],"opportunity_rows":[{"id":"cvea-valdez","name":"Copper Valley Electric Association (Valdez and Glennallen)","sector":"Electric Utilities","region":"Alaska","load":"An islanded cooperative serving about 3,800 members; two 15 MWt units studied to replace the Valdez cogeneration unit","status":"tabled","buyer_model":"cooperative","grid_note":"Islanded cooperative grid, now building a Railbelt intertie via the Alyeska Valdez Marine Terminal","documented":"A completed pre-feasibility study found a micro modular reactor a match for the cooperative's needs, with cooperative ownership beating vendor ownership, year-round heat sales pivotal and federal tax credits worth 30–50% of capital. The board tabled the project indefinitely in August 2023, and the vendor, USNC, entered Chapter 11 in 2024. Alaska's SB 177 removed the legislative siting requirement in 2022. The board's own release says it tabled the project on economics: too much risk for the members to own and operate it, and the vendor could not make an owner-operator PPA pencil without additional year-round heat or power customers. CVEA's September 2023 Ruralite article leaves the door open and names Westinghouse, Oklo, NuScale and Radiant as vendors the cooperative had talked to.","sale_shape":"Cooperative-owned unit in the rate base, with heat sold to a Valdez offtaker; the study says vendor ownership loses.","sources":[{"label":"CVEA × USNC — Pre-Feasibility Study (public version)","url":"https://www.cvea.org/assets/documents/pdfs/mmr/CVEA_Alaska_FS-RELEASEv01.pdf"},{"label":"CVEA — Potential MMR Project page (tabled status + document set)","url":"https://www.cvea.org/about/project-reports/potential-micro-modular-nuclear-reactor-project.html"},{"label":"CVEA — Micro-Modular Nuclear Reactor Project Tabled Indefinitely by CVEA (press release)","url":"https://www.cvea.org/press/release/2023/08/09/micro-modular-nuclear-reactor-project-tabled-indefinitely-by-cvea.html"},{"label":"CVEA / Ruralite (September 2023) — Micro-Modular Nuclear Reactor Project Tabled","url":"https://www.cvea.org/assets/documents/pdfs/ruralite-articles/2023/september.pdf","quote":"Those included Westinghouse, OKLO, NuScale, & Radiant Technologies","status":"fetched"}],"site_id":"cvea-valdez","prospect_id":"cvea"},{"id":"eielson-daf","name":"Department of the Air Force at Eielson (with Golden Valley Electric Association)","sector":"Defense installations","region":"Fairbanks, Alaska","load":"Up to 5 MWe plus steam heat beside a 23 MW coal-fired plant that is 72 years old","status":"intent-to-award","buyer_model":"federal-installation","grid_note":"On the Railbelt; the base buys its electricity from Golden Valley Electric Association today","documented":"A Notice of Intent to Award to Oklo for a 30-year firm-fixed-price power purchase agreement, contracted through DLA Energy, with the award to follow NRC licensing. No price has been published; the Air Force says it will settle the rate in negotiation.","sale_shape":"Owner-operator sells energy and heat under a long-term PPA to a central buying agency; the base's utility, GVEA, stays as supplier and backup.","sources":[{"label":"Oklo Inc. - Oklo Selected as Intended Awardee to Provide Clean, Reliable Power to Eielson Air Force Base in Alaska","url":"https://oklo.com/newsroom/news-details/2025/Oklo-Selected-as-Intended-Awardee-to-Provide-Clean-Reliable-Power-to-Eielson-Air-Force-Base-in-Alaska/default.aspx"},{"label":"Alaska Public Media — 2023 selection + base power context","url":"https://alaskapublic.org/news/2023-09-07/contractor-selected-to-build-nuclear-power-plant-at-eielson-air-force-base"}],"site_id":"eielson-pilot","prospect_id":"eielson-daf"},{"id":"fort-wainwright-doyon","name":"Doyon Utilities (Fort Wainwright and Fort Greely, Alaska)","sector":"Defense installations","region":"Interior Alaska","load":"Electric, heat, water and wastewater systems at Alaska Army installations; capacity not stated in the award record","status":"concept","buyer_model":"federal-installation","grid_note":"Near the Railbelt but self-contained: Doyon operates the installation's own coal-fired combined heat and power plant, not a GVEA connection","documented":"Doyon operates Fort Wainwright utility systems under a long-term privatization contract. The Army FEIS identified 19 MW electric capacity and 45 MW average heat as system needs, then the Army terminated that upgrade EIS in September 2025 to reevaluate the action. A July 2026 RCA notice seeks monthly electric and heat revenue requirements of $824,912 and $3,363,703 based on a 2025 test year; final approval and fuel cost per MWh remain unverified. Fort Wainwright is an unpaired Janus candidate, not a reactor award.","sale_shape":"Possible utility-contract plant addition or separate Army power purchase, subject to DLA, Doyon and RCA approval; no reactor instrument documented.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Award CONT_AWD_SP060007C8263 (PIID SP0600-07-C-8263), Doyon Utilities LLC, Fort Wainwright AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/CONT_AWD_SP060007C8263_9700_-NONE-_-NONE-/"},{"label":"army.mil — Janus next steps","url":"https://www.army.mil/article/289074/army_announces_next_steps_on_janus_program_for_next_generation_nuclear_energy"},{"label":"10 U.S.C. 2688 — Utility systems: conveyance authority","url":"https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid%3AUSC-prelim-title10-section2688&num=0&edition=prelim"},{"label":"Fairbanks Daily News-Miner — Doyon Utilities claims it loses $1.1 million a month on military contracts","url":"https://www.newsminer.com/doyon-utilities-claims-it-loses-million-a-month-on-military/article_45365207-daaf-5663-be7f-b4ad7d9cc7ed.html"},{"label":"U.S. Army — Army reaches agreement with private industry for nuclear micro-reactors","url":"https://www.army.mil/article/294891/army_reaches_agreement_with_private_industry_for_nuclear_micro_reactors"},{"label":"Alaska RCA — 2026 Doyon utility tariff notice","url":"https://aws.state.ak.us/OnlinePublicNotices/Notices/View.aspx?id=224368"},{"label":"U.S. Army — Fort Wainwright heat and electrical upgrade FEIS","url":"https://home.army.mil/wainwright/2517/3144/3965/Final_Environmental_Impact_Statement_Addressing_Heat_and_Electrical_Upgrades_at_Fort_Wainwright_Alaska.pdf"},{"label":"Federal Register — Army termination of upgrade EIS","url":"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/09/17/2025-17972/notice-of-termination-of-environmental-impact-statement"}],"site_id":"fort-wainwright-doyon","prospect_id":"doyon-utilities"},{"id":"red-dog","name":"Teck and NANA Regional Corporation, Red Dog mine","sector":"Mining","region":"Northwest Alaska","load":"About 25 MW, off-grid and diesel-only, burning roughly 14.6 million gallons a year; an 8.8 MW solar farm is expected to deliver about 2 MW in summer","status":"concept","buyer_model":"behind-the-fence-industrial","grid_note":"Off-grid; the mine runs its own diesel power station with no utility of record","documented":"The sharpest microreactor-shaped load in North America: one 20 MWe unit would replace the whole 24.5 MW diesel station. The landowner is an Alaska Native corporation, the power contractor is TUGLIQ, and the mine's closure is planned around 2032. Teck declined on the record to give a cost when asked about the solar project; the mine burns roughly 40,000 gallons of diesel a day, and the solar project's structure is TUGLIQ building and operating on land partly owned by NANA and selling the power to Teck under a negotiated PPA.","sale_shape":"Third-party-owned generation behind the fence under a term contract, with the unit moving to the next site at closure.","sources":[{"label":"ArcticToday - One of Alaska's flagship mines soon could draw energy from the sun","url":"https://www.arctictoday.com/one-of-alaskas-flagship-mines-soon-could-draw-energy-from-the-sun/"},{"label":"Anchorage Daily News — One of Alaska's flagship mines soon could draw energy from the sun","url":"https://www.adn.com/business-economy/energy/2026/03/22/one-of-alaskas-flagship-mines-soon-could-draw-energy-from-the-sun/"}],"site_id":"red-dog-mine","prospect_id":"red-dog"},{"id":"graphite-creek","name":"Graphite Creek graphite mine","sector":"Mining","region":"Seward Peninsula near Nome, Alaska","load":"15.6 MW connected; 12.5 MW nominal operating electric load; 6.6 MW recovered diesel heat in 2025 feasibility study","status":"concept","buyer_model":"behind-the-fence-industrial","grid_note":"Off-grid; the feasibility study's base case is the mine's own diesel generators, not a utility connection","documented":"The 2025 NI 43-101 feasibility study specifies a 15.6 MW connected load, 12.5 MW nominal operating load, three 7.5 MW diesel generators (two running, one standby), about 25.7 million litres/year of diesel for power, and 6.6 MW of recovered heat. It chose diesel as the base case because an SMR option was not readily available and indicative SMR CHP capital was $80–90 million. It recommends an SMR-versus-diesel tradeoff study costing less than $50,000. No public award or study completion was located. The federal permitting dashboard now shows environmental review in progress with estimated completion September 24, 2027.","sale_shape":null,"sources":[{"label":"Graphite One — Graphite Creek NI 43-101 feasibility study, section 26.12","url":"https://www.graphiteoneinc.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/NI-43101_FS_Report_20250422_Final.pdf","quote":"An initial study of this tradeoff should cost less than $50,000.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"KNOM Radio Mission — Graphite One forecasts $43 billion in revenue from proposed Seward Peninsula mine (2025-04-30)","url":"https://knom.org/2025/04/30/graphite-one-forecasts-43-billion-in-revenue-from-proposed-seward-peninsula-mine/","quote":"the proposed mine would draw 12.5 mW of power","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Mining Technology — Graphite Creek Project, Alaska (project profile)","url":"https://www.mining-technology.com/projects/graphite-creek-project-alaska/","quote":"The mine operations are expected to require 4MW of power, which would increase to 6MW after five years.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"U.S. Permitting Dashboard — Graphite Creek","url":"https://www.permits.performance.gov/permitting-project/fast-41-covered-projects/graphite-creek-project","quote":"09/24/2027","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Graphite One — news releases through September 2026","url":"https://www.graphiteoneinc.com/news/"}],"site_id":"graphite-creek","prospect_id":null},{"id":"tanana-chiefs","name":"Tanana Chiefs Conference and the Alaska Village Electric Cooperative","sector":"Electric Utilities","region":"Interior Alaska","load":"Eight village microgrids, each far below 1 MW, aggregated under a tribal energy-sovereignty award","status":"evidence-only","buyer_model":"cooperative","grid_note":"Eight isolated village microgrids with no interconnection between them; no single village comes close to a microreactor's minimum output on its own","documented":"A $32.6M DOE award, $26.1M federal, is retrofitting solar and storage into eight villages that run on diesel for 100% of their electricity, targeting a 40% cut. It creates an eight-village tribal power entity. A separate $5.0M award at Kokhanok buys about $5,000/kW of non-diesel capacity for a 170-person village.","sale_shape":"Tribal-utility-owned unit at a hub village with public capital, inside the PCE settlement.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Assistance award ASST_NON_DECD0000069 (FAIN DECD0000069), Dena Nena Henash (Tanana Chiefs Conference), AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/ASST_NON_DECD0000069_089/"},{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Assistance award ASST_NON_DECD0000120 (FAIN DECD0000120), Kokhanok Village, Lake and Peninsula AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/ASST_NON_DECD0000120_089/"}],"site_id":null,"prospect_id":"tanana-chiefs"}],"precedent_rows":[{"id":"gvea-aurora-chena-2026-ppa-amendment","name":"GVEA-Aurora Chena power purchase agreement amendment","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Fairbanks, Alaska","parties":{"host":"Golden Valley Electric Association","provider":"Aurora Energy, LLC","utility":"Golden Valley Electric Association"},"instrument":"PPA","capacity":null,"price":"$115.23/MWh base rate for 2026, up from $94.23/MWh; 2.8% annual escalation for the remaining term","price_status":"proposed","capex":null,"signed":"2026","term_years":5,"displaced":null,"summary":"GVEA filed an amendment to its existing Aurora Energy wholesale PPA and asked the RCA for approval by February 23, 2026, with a March 1 effective date. The proposed amendment pays $115.23/MWh in 2026, escalates 2.8% annually, and supports repairs intended to restore 150,000 MWh of annual output. The public notice does not establish that the Commission approved the amendment, so the economics remain proposed rather than final.","microreactor_read":"A firm 1-20 MW reactor serving Interior Alaska must compete with a filed dispatchable-power benchmark of $115.23/MWh plus 2.8% annual escalation, while matching the PPA's dependable annual volume.","sources":[{"label":"State of Alaska / RCA - TA401-13 GVEA-Aurora tariff filing notice (2026)","url":"https://aws.state.ak.us/OnlinePublicNotices/Notices/View.aspx?id=222446","quote":"1 sets a new base rate for 2026 of $115.23/MWh, an increase from the current 2026 rate of $94.23/MWh.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"mea-energy49-houston-solar-ppa","name":"MEA-Energy 49 Houston solar power purchase agreement","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Houston, Alaska","parties":{"host":"Matanuska Electric Association","provider":"Energy 49, LLC","utility":"Matanuska Electric Association"},"instrument":"PPA","capacity":"6 MW solar; MEA commits to purchase 100% of metered output","price":"$0.06700/kWh in year one, with 1.5% annual inflation increase","price_status":"proposed","capex":null,"signed":"2022","term_years":25,"displaced":null,"summary":"MEA filed a 25-year PPA for all output from Energy 49's 6 MW Houston solar project. The initial price is $67/MWh with 1.5% annual escalation; delivery was required within three years of execution. The agreement also splits REC economic benefits equally and allows termination after an uncured material breach. The fetched notice requests approval and warns the Commission may vary the rate, so this record does not claim final approval.","microreactor_read":"This is an Alaska unit-size filing comparator: a signed 6 MW solar PPA was filed at $67/MWh initially for 25 years, but final RCA approval was not verified. A reactor offer would need its own negotiated PPA or special contract and approval, with firm-output and nuclear risks priced explicitly.","sources":[{"label":"State of Alaska / RCA - TA535-18 MEA-Energy 49 PPA notice (2022)","url":"https://aws.state.ak.us/OnlinePublicNotices/Notices/View.aspx?id=205057","quote":"MEA agrees to pay Energy 49 an initial rate of $0.06700/kWh for all energy delivered to MEA during the first year","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"eielson-afb-oklo-microreactor-noita","name":"Eielson Air Force Base microreactor pilot - Notice of Intent to Award to Oklo","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Eielson AFB, Alaska","parties":{"host":"Department of the Air Force / 354th Fighter Wing","provider":"Oklo Inc. (Aurora powerhouse)","utility":"Defense Logistics Agency Energy (contracting agency)"},"instrument":"PPA","capacity":"Up to 5 MWe plus steam heat, alongside an existing coal-fired plant with a maximum capacity of 23 MW","price":"Not disclosed. Reporting states the Air Force will settle the price of the contract and the rate it pays for power during negotiations.","price_status":null,"capex":null,"signed":"2025-06","term_years":30,"displaced":"Supplements Eielson's existing coal-fired power plant (23 MW max capacity) and the base's winter heating fuel burn","summary":"On 11 June 2025 the Defense Logistics Agency Energy, acting for the Department of the Air Force, issued a Notice of Intent to Award to Oklo for the DoD's first commercial microreactor. The structure is a build-own-operate arrangement: Oklo would design, construct, own and operate the plant and sell electricity and steam to the base under a long-term, firm-fixed-price power purchase agreement reported at 30 years. Capacity is up to 5 MWe, sitting alongside Eielson's existing 23 MW coal-fired plant. No price has been published; the rate for power is explicitly left to contract negotiation, and the award is contingent on Oklo obtaining an NRC license.","microreactor_read":"This is the cleanest template for selling a 1-20 MW reactor into a remote outpost: the host buys energy, not a reactor, on a 30-year firm-fixed-price PPA from a private owner-operator, contracted by a central buying agency (DLA Energy) rather than the base. The commercially important gap is that no price is published, so the deal proves the instrument exists but sets no benchmark - and the reactor is sized at 5 MW against a 23 MW incumbent, i.e. it is a supplement, not a replacement.","sources":[{"label":"Oklo Inc. - Oklo Selected as Intended Awardee to Provide Clean, Reliable Power to Eielson Air Force Base in Alaska","url":"https://oklo.com/newsroom/news-details/2025/Oklo-Selected-as-Intended-Awardee-to-Provide-Clean-Reliable-Power-to-Eielson-Air-Force-Base-in-Alaska/default.aspx","quote":"Oklo would design, construct, own, and operate the power plant, delivering both electricity and heat","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Air & Space Forces Magazine - Air Force Moves Ahead with Military's First Microreactor","url":"https://www.airandspaceforces.com/air-force-microreactor-eielson-alaska/","quote":"existing coal-fired power plant, which has a max capacity of 23 megawatts","status":"fetched"},{"label":"POWER Magazine - Oklo Retapped for Alaska Air Force Microreactor","url":"https://www.powermag.com/oklo-retapped-for-alaska-air-force-microreactor-proposes-nuclear-fleet-wide-licensing-framework/","quote":"deliver both electricity and heat under a 30-year, firm-fixed-price PPA","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"red-dog-mine-solar-teck-nana-tugliq","name":"Red Dog Mine Solar Farm + Battery","sector":"Off-grid mining & mineral processing","region":"De Long Mountains, Northwest Arctic Borough, Alaska","parties":{"host":"Teck Alaska Incorporated (Red Dog zinc-lead mine), NANA Regional Corporation as landowner","provider":"TUGLIQ Energy","utility":"None - off-grid"},"instrument":"PPA (terms under negotiation as of March 2026)","capacity":"8.8 MW solar farm on ~30 acres plus a battery system; expected to deliver about 2 MW in summer against a site load of roughly 25 MW","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":null,"signed":"2026","term_years":null,"displaced":"Diesel at a mine burning roughly 40,000 gallons per day (about 14.6 million gallons/yr) for a 24.5 MW on-site diesel plant","summary":"Red Dog would be the first large Alaskan mine to take substantial on-site solar. TUGLIQ - the same Quebec firm behind Raglan - would build and operate an 8.8 MW array plus battery on 30 acres partly on NANA land, with Teck negotiating power-purchase terms rather than buying the plant. Teck declined to give a cost estimate. The scale gap is the story: 8.8 MW nameplate yields about 2 MW in summer against a site that needs roughly 25 MW and burns about 40,000 gallons of diesel a day, and the build is being rushed to start by June 2026 to catch a federal tax-credit deadline in July. The mine is projected to close around 2032.","microreactor_read":"Red Dog is the sharpest microreactor-shaped load in North America: ~25 MW, off-grid, diesel-only, and a solar answer that covers under a tenth of it. A single 20 MWe unit replaces the entire 24.5 MW diesel station. Two hard constraints: the incumbent price is the delivered cost of ~14.6 million gallons of barged diesel a year (Alaskan remote power is commonly quoted at 40 cents to over $1.00/kWh), and closure around 2032 means the economic window for a fixed asset has already shut - only a relocatable unit fits.","sources":[{"label":"ArcticToday - One of Alaska's flagship mines soon could draw energy from the sun","url":"https://www.arctictoday.com/one-of-alaskas-flagship-mines-soon-could-draw-energy-from-the-sun/","quote":"8.8-megawatt solar farm","status":"fetched"},{"label":"ArcticToday - One of Alaska's flagship mines soon could draw energy from the sun (diesel burn)","url":"https://www.arctictoday.com/one-of-alaskas-flagship-mines-soon-could-draw-energy-from-the-sun/","quote":"roughly 40,000 gallons each day","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"graphite-one-graphite-creek-power-plan","name":"Graphite One / Graphite Creek Project power plan","sector":"Off-grid mining & mineral processing","region":"Seward Peninsula (Nome), Alaska","parties":{"host":"Graphite One Inc.","provider":"not yet selected"},"instrument":null,"capacity":"12.5 MW forecast draw per 2025 reporting (an earlier feasibility study cited 4 MW rising to 6 MW); default plan is a trio of diesel generators","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":null,"signed":null,"term_years":null,"displaced":null,"summary":"Graphite One's Graphite Creek graphite mine on Alaska's Seward Peninsula, targeting first production around 2030, is forecast to draw 12.5 MW of power -- 'over twice the amount of power the entire city of Nome draws' -- against an earlier feasibility-study estimate of 4 MW rising to 6 MW after five years. The company's leading proposal remains a trio of diesel generators (one on standby), but its own SVP of Operations named nuclear power as a genuine option in April 2025 reporting, alongside wind (strong resource, long lead time) and solar (good in summer only), citing zero carbon footprint against two conditions: vendor maturity and social acceptability. No vendor, RFP or financing decision has been made for any option.","microreactor_read":"Graphite One is the clearest pre-construction Alaska mine naming nuclear power as an option, at a forecast load (12.5 MW) inside the 1-20 MW band; a vendor has a multi-year window before 2030 production to prove a design mature and accepted enough to displace the company's default diesel-genset plan.","sources":[{"label":"KNOM Radio Mission — Graphite One forecasts $43 billion in revenue from proposed Seward Peninsula mine (2025-04-30)","url":"https://knom.org/2025/04/30/graphite-one-forecasts-43-billion-in-revenue-from-proposed-seward-peninsula-mine/","quote":"the proposed mine would draw 12.5 mW of power","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Mining Technology — Graphite Creek Project, Alaska (project profile)","url":"https://www.mining-technology.com/projects/graphite-creek-project-alaska/","quote":"The mine operations are expected to require 4MW of power, which would increase to 6MW after five years.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"kokhanok-paradigm-shift-era-microgrid","name":"Kokhanok Paradigm Shift microgrid upgrade, DOE ERA (DE-CD0000120)","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Kokhanok, Lake and Peninsula Borough, Alaska (Iliamna Lake)","parties":{"host":"Kokhanok Village Council","provider":"U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations (funder)","utility":"Kokhanok Village Council (village utility, PCE participant PC59)"},"instrument":"grant-funded","capacity":"1 MW / 943 kWh battery energy storage system, plus 100 kW solar PV, a new 100 kW wind turbine and electric thermal storage heating units, added to the village's existing diesel-supplemented microgrid","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":"$4,969,257 federal obligation, no non-federal cost share; $1,567,876 outlaid as of 2026-07-14","signed":"2024-08","term_years":4,"displaced":"70 percent or more of the village power plant's fossil fuel use within the first two years of operation, per DOE. Alaska RCA's FY2024 rate filing puts the village's actual load at 358,953 kWh test-period sales (roughly 41 kW average) and its certified cost of service at $1.0209/kWh, of which $0.6498/kWh is fuel — a fuel bill of roughly $233,000/year the grant is displacing.","summary":"OCED awarded Kokhanok Village $4,969,257 on 2024-08-13 under the ERA program to add a 1 MW/943 kWh battery, 100 kW solar, a 100 kW wind turbine and electric thermal storage to the microgrid of a 170-resident Alutiiq, Dena'ina and Yup'ik village reachable only by barge and plane. DOE's own write-up says the upgrade would displace 70 percent of the local power plant's diesel within two years. Federal money covers 100 percent of it, and about a third of the award had already been outlaid by mid-2026, so this is a project in construction rather than a paper selection. The load being served is genuinely tiny: Alaska RCA's own FY2024 filing puts Kokhanok's test-period sales at 358,953 kWh, i.e. roughly a 41 kW average load, against a certified cost of service of $1.0209/kWh ($0.6498/kWh of which is fuel).","microreactor_read":"Five million dollars of federal money for a village of 170 people (roughly $29,200 per resident) is buying about $5,000 per installed kW of non-diesel capacity to displace $0.6498/kWh diesel fuel — a willingness to pay, and a capex benchmark, that no commercial power market would support without a grant. Against a 41 kW average load, a 1 MW-class microreactor would be roughly 20x oversized for this single village: Kokhanok prices the sector's grant economics and simultaneously proves the smallest microreactor is still too big for the single-village case.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Assistance award ASST_NON_DECD0000120 (FAIN DECD0000120), Kokhanok Village, Lake and Peninsula AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/ASST_NON_DECD0000120_089/","quote":"THIS PROJECT SEEKS TO INSTALL A 943 KWH BATTERY ENERGY STORAGE SYSTEM","status":"fetched"},{"label":"DOE Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations — Award Wednesdays, August 14, 2024","url":"https://www.energy.gov/oced/articles/award-wednesdays-august-14-2024","quote":"would displace an anticipated 70% of the local power plant’s diesel use within the first two years of operation","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Regulatory Commission of Alaska — Letter Order L2300184, Appendix 1 for Kokhanok Village Council","url":"https://pce.akenergyauthority.org/Documents/FY24%20Base%20Rate%20Change%20Final%20L2300184.pdf","quote":"Kokhanok Village Council APPENDIX 1 Power Cost Equalization Calculation Base Rate Change to $0.1935","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"avec-new-stuyahok-ekwok-era-solar-storage","name":"New Stuyahok and Ekwok solar-plus-storage, DOE Energy Improvements in Rural or Remote Areas (DE-CD0000119)","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"New Stuyahok, Alaska (Dillingham Census Area)","parties":{"host":"Alaska Village Electric Cooperative, on behalf of New Stuyahok and Ekwok","provider":"U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations (funder)","utility":"Alaska Village Electric Cooperative"},"instrument":"grant-funded","capacity":"500 kW solar PV array + 540 kWh battery energy storage system + microgrid controller, integrated with the existing village diesel power plant","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":"$4,340,079 federal obligation, no non-federal cost share, so total project funding is also $4,340,079. Only $36,013 had been outlaid as of the record's last modification on 2026-07-20.","signed":"2024-09","term_years":3,"displaced":"An estimated 24 percent of the fuel burned at the existing diesel power plant serving New Stuyahok and Ekwok","summary":"DOE's Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations awarded Alaska Village Electric Cooperative $4,340,079 on 2024-09-24 under the Energy Improvements in Rural or Remote Areas program, to build a 500 kW solar array, a 540 kWh battery and a microgrid controller for the Yup'ik villages of New Stuyahok and Ekwok in the Dillingham region of southwest Alaska. The federal share is 100 percent — the award record shows no non-federal funding at all — and the stated benefit is a 24 percent cut in the existing diesel plant's fuel consumption, plus a reduction in the volume of fuel barged up the Nushagak River. This is the first record in this file to come from the ERA program, and its shape is the ERA shape: a grant, not a supply contract, with no price per kilowatt-hour anywhere in it.","microreactor_read":"The award buys 500 kW of solar nameplate in southwest Alaska for $4.34M — $8,680 per kW of a resource that produces nothing in December — and gets a 24 percent diesel cut for it. Scaled linearly off the award's own numbers, a full diesel displacement at these two villages is worth roughly $18M of public money, which is the ceiling a 1 MW microreactor has to sit under; the reactor's real advantage is that it is the only item on that list that supplies firm capacity in the dark months, when the PV contributes zero and the diesel contributes everything.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Assistance award ASST_NON_DECD0000119 (FAIN DECD0000119), Alaska Village Electric Cooperative Inc., New Stuyahok AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/ASST_NON_DECD0000119_089/","quote":"THIS PROJECT PROPOSES TO CONSTRUCT A 500 KW SOLAR PHOTOVOLTAIC (PV) ARRAY, A 540 KWH BATTERY ENERGY","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"tanana-chiefs-alaskan-tribal-energy-sovereignty-era","name":"Alaskan Tribal Energy Sovereignty — eight-village microgrid upgrade, DOE ERA (DE-CD0000069)","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Nulato, Huslia, Minto, Kaltag, Grayling, Anvik, Shageluk and Holy Cross, Alaska","parties":{"host":"Tanana Chiefs Conference (Dena Nena Henash), with Alaska Village Electric Cooperative","provider":"U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations (funder)"},"instrument":"grant-funded","capacity":"High-penetration solar PV and battery storage retrofitted into eight existing village microgrids; individual MW ratings not stated in the award record","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":"$26,062,370 federal cost share plus $6,521,843 non-federal, $32,584,213 total funding. DOE released $4.1 million of that for Phase 1 activities; USAspending's obligation field carries the full multi-phase federal share, and total outlays stood at $431,606 as of 2026-08-14.","signed":"2024-08","term_years":6,"displaced":"Eight communities that rely on diesel for 100 percent of their electricity; the project targets a 40 percent cut in regional diesel consumption and more than 1,550 tonnes a year of avoided CO2","summary":"OCED awarded the Tanana Chiefs Conference a cooperative agreement on 2024-08-13 under the ERA program to put solar and storage into the existing microgrids of eight roadless interior Alaska villages, all of which currently generate 100 percent of their electricity from diesel and pay more than four times the national average for it. The federal share is $26,062,370 against $6,521,843 of non-federal money, for $32,584,213 total — about $4.07 million per village — and the goal is a 40 percent regional diesel offset. The obligated-versus-potential distinction here is unusually sharp and the award record hides it: USAspending reports $26,062,370 as the obligation, while DOE's own award announcement says only 'more than $4.1 million' was released to begin Phase 1. The stated end-state is Alaska's largest tribally owned independent power producer.","microreactor_read":"$4.07M of public money per village buys a 40 percent diesel cut, which extrapolates to roughly $10.2M per village for full displacement — the price band a microreactor would have to hit at any one of these eight sites. The binding constraint is not that number but the load: a village whose whole electrical demand is served by a small diesel plant is well below the 1 MW floor of the smallest microreactor, so the addressable buyer is the eight-village tribal IPP this award creates, not any single village.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Assistance award ASST_NON_DECD0000069 (FAIN DECD0000069), Dena Nena Henash (Tanana Chiefs Conference), AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/ASST_NON_DECD0000069_089/","quote":"MICROGRIDS IN EIGHT REMOTE TRIBAL COMMUNITIES CURRENTLY RELYING ON DIESEL FOR 100% OF THEIR","status":"fetched"},{"label":"DOE Office of Clean Energy Demonstrations — Award Wednesdays, August 14, 2024","url":"https://www.energy.gov/oced/articles/award-wednesdays-august-14-2024","quote":"with more than $4.1 million (of the total federal cost share of up to $26.06 million) to begin Phase 1 activities","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"doyon-utilities-alaska-utility-privatization","name":"Doyon Utilities 50-year utility privatization, Alaska Army installations (SP0600-07-C-8263)","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Fort Wainwright, Alaska (plus Fort Greely and JBER-Richardson under the Doyon Utilities program)","parties":{"host":"U.S. Army, contracting through DLA Energy","provider":"Doyon Utilities, LLC (Alaska Native corporation joint venture)","utility":"Doyon Utilities, LLC — regulated by the Regulatory Commission of Alaska"},"instrument":"utility tariff","capacity":"Full electric, heat, water and wastewater utility systems at Alaska Army installations; capacity not stated in the award record","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":"$894,733,810 obligated to date against a base-and-all-options value of $3,082,072,958; period of performance 2007-09-28 to 2058-08-14","signed":"2007-09","term_years":51,"displaced":"Government ownership and operation of installation utility systems in interior Alaska, where the incumbent generation is coal and oil-fired central plant","summary":"DLA Energy awarded Doyon Utilities a utility privatization contract on 2007-09-28 covering Army installation utility systems in Alaska, with a period of performance that runs to 2058-08-14 — a fifty-one year term. The award record shows $894,733,810 already obligated and a base-and-all-options value of $3,082,072,958, making this by a wide margin the largest single utility vehicle surfaced in this sweep. A companion contract to the same recipient on the same day (SP0600-07-C-8261) carries a further $212,665,748 obligated. The contractor is a regulated utility: its rates at these installations are set by the Regulatory Commission of Alaska, not by the contract alone.","microreactor_read":"This is the instrument a microreactor should be sold into rather than around: a single counterparty already holds a fifty-year, three-billion-dollar franchise to supply heat and power to Alaska Army installations, and its generation is coal and oil in a place where fuel arrives by rail and barge. The reactor does not need to beat a $/kW number to enter — it needs to become an asset inside a rate base a regulator already approves, and the addressable line is the roughly $60M a year of obligations this one contract has averaged since 2007.","sources":[{"label":"USAspending.gov API — Award CONT_AWD_SP060007C8263 (PIID SP0600-07-C-8263), Doyon Utilities LLC, Fort Wainwright AK","url":"https://api.usaspending.gov/api/v2/awards/CONT_AWD_SP060007C8263_9700_-NONE-_-NONE-/","quote":"UTILITY SERVICE CONTRACT FOR UTILITY PRIVATIZATION","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"alaska-pce-fy2024-base-rate-order","name":"Alaska Power Cost Equalization - FY2024 base rate order and per-utility cost of service","sector":"Remote outposts & microgrids","region":"Statewide rural Alaska","parties":{"host":"193 rural Alaska communities (~82,000 residents)","provider":"Alaska Energy Authority (program administrator)","utility":"Regulatory Commission of Alaska (rate-setter)"},"instrument":"utility tariff","capacity":"Statewide program covering the diesel-dominated rural utilities; individual utilities range from ~50 MWh/yr (Lime Village) to ~19.7 GWh/yr (Kotzebue Electric Association) of test-period sales","price":"PCE base rate set at $0.1935/kWh effective 2023-07-01 (down from $0.1958/kWh); statutory maximum eligible cost is $1.00/kWh less the base rate, i.e. $0.8065/kWh of PCE credit","price_status":null,"capex":null,"signed":"2023-06","term_years":null,"displaced":"RCA Appendix 1 cost-of-service calculations for FY2024: Igiugig Village Council total power cost $1.0944/kWh of which fuel/purchased power is $0.8140/kWh; Kokhanok Village Council $1.0209/kWh total, $0.6498/kWh fuel; Chitina Electric $1.0669/kWh total, $0.4015/kWh fuel; Lime Village $1.9487/kWh total, $1.5288/kWh fuel; Galena $0.6242/kWh total, $0.3813/kWh fuel; Kotzebue Electric Association $0.4079/kWh total, $0.1885/kWh fuel","summary":"The Regulatory Commission of Alaska set the FY2024 Power Cost Equalization base rate at $0.1935/kWh on 13 June 2023 and, a week later, recalculated the per-kWh PCE credit for every participating rural utility. The attached Appendix 1 sheets are the single best published price-to-beat dataset in US remote power: for each utility they state test-period kWh sales, non-fuel power cost per kWh, fuel/purchased-power cost per kWh, and total power cost per kWh. Total power costs run from about $0.41/kWh at wind-equipped Kotzebue to $1.9487/kWh at Lime Village, with fuel alone above $0.81/kWh at Igiugig and $1.52/kWh at Lime Village. The state pays the gap above the base rate up to a statutory ceiling of $1.00/kWh of total cost, so any community whose costs exceed roughly $1.00/kWh is already eating unsubsidised cost.","microreactor_read":"This is the price sheet. A 1-20 MW reactor selling into rural Alaska must beat a delivered cost of service that the regulator itself certifies at $0.62-$1.95/kWh, with the fuel-only component alone at $0.38-$1.53/kWh in the villages - but the loads are tiny (Igiugig 272 MWh/yr, Kokhanok 359 MWh/yr), so only aggregated or hub communities like Kotzebue (19.7 GWh/yr, and already down at $0.41/kWh because of wind) present a load a reactor could actually serve. The commercially interesting band is the mid-size hub at $0.60-$1.00/kWh, not the cheapest and not the smallest.","sources":[{"label":"Regulatory Commission of Alaska - Letter Order L2300184, FY2024 PCE base rate change","url":"https://pce.akenergyauthority.org/Documents/FY24%20Base%20Rate%20Change%20Final%20L2300184.pdf","quote":"On June 13, 2023, the Commission established a new base rate of 19.35 cents per kilowatt-hour (kWh) for PCE calculations.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Alaska Energy Authority - Power Cost Equalization programme page","url":"https://akenergyauthority.org/What-We-Do/Power-Cost-Equalization","quote":"serves 82,000 Alaskans in 193 communities that are largely reliant on diesel fuel for power generation","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"unisea-dutch-harbor-waste-heat-to-power","name":"UniSea seafood plant, Unalaska/Dutch Harbor, Alaska - ICE Thermal Harvesting waste-heat-to-power system","sector":"Agriculture & Food","region":"Unalaska, Alaska","parties":{"host":"UniSea, Inc.","provider":"ICE Thermal Harvesting, LLC","utility":"City of Unalaska (municipal electric utility)"},"instrument":"EaaS","capacity":"300 kW","price":null,"price_status":null,"capex":null,"signed":"2025-09","term_years":null,"displaced":"diesel generation - approximately 3,500 gallons per week of diesel projected to be displaced during the processing season","summary":"On September 4, 2025, ICE Thermal Harvesting and UniSea launched what the companies described as the Alaska seafood industry's first waste-heat-to-power system at UniSea's Dutch Harbor/Unalaska processing plant, a 300 kW unit that captures waste heat from UniSea's existing diesel generators and converts it to electricity rather than adding new generation. In its first three weeks of operation the system produced 67 MWh and displaced roughly 5,000 gallons of diesel; UniSea projected ongoing savings of about 3,500 gallons of diesel per week during the processing season. Financial terms (capex, contract price, term) were not disclosed in the announcement.","microreactor_read":"300 kW is far below the 1-20 MW reactor floor, but it is the clearest real, dated, priced-in-diesel-gallons evidence that remote seafood plants on an isolated community grid actively invest in on-site generation to cut diesel burn - the same value proposition a microreactor would sell, just at roughly 1/10th to 1/60th the scale of this one plant's demonstrated appetite for displacing diesel.","sources":[{"label":"PR Newswire - ICE Thermal Harvesting and UniSea Launch Alaska Seafood Industry's First Waste Heat to Power System","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/ice-thermal-harvesting-and-unisea-launch-alaska-seafood-industrys-first-waste-heat-to-power-system-302546873.html","quote":"the system will generate 300kW and replace ~3500 gallons of diesel usage each week during the processing seasons","status":"fetched"}]}],"precedent_rows_priced":10};window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('mr:alaska'));
